# run-issues-poller.env.example — machine configuration for poller.sh,
# pr-watch-poller.sh, status-render.sh AND action-server.sh (all four
# LaunchAgents source it).
#
# Copy to $HOME/.config/run-issues/poller.env (or point
# RUN_ISSUES_POLLER_ENV_FILE somewhere else) and edit. Nothing here is
# required: with no file at all both pollers keep their built-in defaults.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#   launchd hands an agent no environment of its own, and the login files hold
#   nothing run-issues-specific, so under a LaunchAgent this file is the ONLY
#   channel through which a machine can configure its pollers. It is sourced by
#   the poller, which means THE FILE WINS over a variable inherited from the
#   environment — a value set here overrides one exported in a shell.
#
# NO SECRET MATERIAL HERE
#   Tokens and private keys belong in $HOME/.config/run-issues/env (chmod 600),
#   which orchestrate.sh and pr-watch.sh source themselves. The pollers
#   deliberately never read that file: they log copiously and must not carry a
#   token. Keep this file to paths, host names and IDENTITY config.
#
#   The GitHub App identity block below (issue #127) is the one thing beyond
#   paths/hosts, and it is NOT secret material: an App ID, an installation ID and
#   a PATH to the private key. The key itself — the .pem — stays a 0600 file on
#   disk and is only referenced by path, exactly as it is in env. That is why the
#   poller may read it here even though a token never may.

# Which machines may run the pollers. Comma- and/or whitespace-separated shell
# glob patterns, matched against `hostname -s`. `*` allows every host.
#
# Unset, the pollers fall back to a built-in list of the hosts they ran on
# before the gate became configurable, so an existing machine keeps working
# without this file. On any other machine a poller that matches nothing exits 0
# and touches nothing — that is the fail-safe direction, and it is why an
# unedited copy of this file is harmless.
RUN_ISSUES_POLLER_HOSTS="<this-machine-hostname>"

# Where the four log files per poller go
# (<poller>.log, <poller>.runs.log, <poller>.stdout.log, <poller>.stderr.log).
# The plists carry no StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath keys — launchd performs
# no variable expansion in those — so the poller owns every one of its log
# paths, and this one variable moves all of them.
RUN_ISSUES_LOG_DIR="$HOME/Library/Logs"

# Watchlist location. Leave this unset unless the watchlist really lives
# somewhere unusual: unset, the pollers try $HOME/.config/run-issues/watchlist.json
# and then the legacy dotfiles path, in that order.
#
# CAUTION: setting this DISABLES that fallback chain. An explicit override that
# points at a missing file stops the poller with a "watchlist missing" log line
# rather than quietly polling a different repo set than you asked for.
#RUN_ISSUES_WATCHLIST="$HOME/.config/run-issues/watchlist.json"

# Seconds without a state.jsonl event after which a run is treated as stalled:
# its tmux session is killed and the run finalized as blocked. Must stay above
# the longest legitimate single-phase claude call.
#RUN_ISSUES_STALE_AFTER=3600

# Label that marks a finished issue for automatic teardown.
#RUN_ISSUES_CLEAN_LABEL=auto-clean

# Log rotation threshold in bytes (default 10 MB). At the start of each tick,
# before writing anything, a poller rotates any of its four log files that has
# grown past this to <file>.1, keeping a single previous generation. Set 0 to
# disable rotation entirely. Nothing rotates the run-dir state.jsonl files —
# those shrink only when a run-dir is cleaned up.
#RUN_ISSUES_LOG_MAX_BYTES=10485760

# pr-watch-poller.sh ONLY: a separate concurrency cap for the PR watcher. A PR
# scan is seconds of work, not the tens of minutes an orchestrator run takes, so
# it can safely run at a higher cap than poller.sh without widening orchestrator
# concurrency. Unset, it falls back to the watchlist key pr_watch_max_concurrent,
# then to global_max_concurrent — so leaving it out keeps the current behaviour.
# Set here, it wins over the watchlist key. See CLAUDE.md section 7.
#PR_WATCH_GLOBAL_MAX=6

# status-render.sh ONLY (#78): when the status-page LaunchAgent regenerates the
# page, drive status.sh with --github so the page shows issue TITLES (as each
# row's main text) and CI / merge-readiness chips on open-PR rows. Fail-soft: if
# the enrichment run fails outright, the page still renders with local data.
# Recommended default for an install: 1. Set 0 for a pure local page.
#
#   SECURITY: issue titles reveal client/project context far more plainly than a
#   repo slug or branch name. Only enable this while the page is served on a
#   private tailnet — never behind a public tunnel. See README section 7.8.
RUN_ISSUES_RENDER_GITHUB=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ohjaamo action service (action-server.sh) — issue #77. OPT-IN mutation
# channel: four buttons on the status page (Pysäytä / Siivoa / Salli auto-merge
# / Jatka), each a thin shell over an existing script or label.
#
#   SECURITY (README section 7.9): the service binds ONLY to a Tailscale address
#   (never 0.0.0.0), authenticates the caller by tailnet identity (tailscale
#   whois), and must NOT sit behind a reverse proxy. Keep the page + service on
#   the tailnet only — the buttons mutate live orchestration.
#
# Leave the whole block commented to keep the status page a pure read surface.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which machines may run the action service. Same format/semantics as
# RUN_ISSUES_POLLER_HOSTS; no match => the service exits 0 and binds nothing.
#RUN_ISSUES_ACTION_HOSTS="<this-machine-hostname>"

# The service base URL AS THE BROWSER REACHES IT (host:8081, the Tailscale name
# or IP the status page is opened against). Setting this is what makes
# status-render.sh embed the buttons + the shared token; unset => no buttons.
#RUN_ISSUES_ACTION_BASE="http://<this-machine>:8081"

# Allowed page origin(s) for the CSRF Origin check (CSV). Default http://<bind>:8080.
# Set this to exactly the origin the status page is served from.
#RUN_ISSUES_ACTION_ORIGIN="http://<this-machine>:8080"

# Allowed tailnet users (CSV of LoginName). Default: this node's own owner — so
# the same person from phone/laptop passes too (trust boundary = user, not device).
#RUN_ISSUES_ACTION_ALLOWED_USERS="you@github"

# Bind address / port. Default bind = the first `tailscale ip -4`; default port 8081.
# Leave unset in production (a wildcard bind is refused). Tests use 127.0.0.1.
#RUN_ISSUES_ACTION_BIND=
#RUN_ISSUES_ACTION_PORT=8081

# Overrides for the Tailscale CLI and the Python interpreter, if not auto-found.
#RUN_ISSUES_TAILSCALE_BIN=/Applications/Tailscale.app/Contents/MacOS/Tailscale
#RUN_ISSUES_ACTION_PYTHON=/usr/bin/python3

# --- self-update.sh (issue #112) --------------------------------------------
# The self-update LaunchAgent (StartInterval 3600) keeps the installed package
# current: on a dev clone it pulls origin/main (guarded, ff-only) then runs
# install.sh; on a maintainer submodule the pull is always skipped and only
# install.sh runs. No host gate — opt-in is bootstrapping the agent. TRUST
# BOUNDARY: an unattended pull runs whatever was merged into main on the next
# tick; the review gate is the PR review, not this install (README §7.10).
# Kill switch: set to 0 to disable the tick entirely.
#RUN_ISSUES_SELF_UPDATE=0

# --- GitHub App identity for the poller's own reads (issue #127) -------------
# The poller's per-tick LIST reads — pickup (pick_oldest_candidate), the epic
# scan (epic_list_open) and the clean scan's label query — route through the
# GitHub App when these are set, so their volume spends the App's own 15k/h rate
# limit instead of maintainer's personal quota. Without them the poller falls straight
# through to bare `gh` (bit-for-bit the pre-#127 behaviour) — the App is never an
# install requirement.
#
# NOT SECRET MATERIAL (see the header): an App ID, an installation ID and a PATH.
# The private key (.pem) stays a 0600 file on disk; only its path lives here. Set
# the SAME three values in $HOME/.config/run-issues/env too, so orchestrate.sh /
# pr-watch.sh (which never source poller.env) authenticate the same App for the
# writes and the run-time reads. A non-origin remote always bypasses the App (the
# installation is per-org).
#RUN_ISSUES_GITHUB_APP_ID=<numeric app id>
#RUN_ISSUES_GITHUB_APP_INSTALLATION_ID=<numeric installation id>
#RUN_ISSUES_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH="$HOME/.config/run-issues/app.pem"
